}

/**
 * @param {SimpleCommand} c
 * @returns {string | null} why the command is destructive
 */
function destructive(c) {
  if (c.name === 'git') return gitDestructive(c.args)
  if (c.name === 'rm') {
    const opts = beforeDashDash(c.args)
    if (shortFlags(opts).has('r') || shortFlags(opts).has('R') || opts.includes('--recursive')) return 'rm -r deletes whole directory trees'
  }
  return null
}

/**
 * Why a shell command would destroy work or history, or null if it would not.
 *
 * @param {string} command
 * @returns {string | null}
 */
export function guard(command) {
  for (const c of parseCommands(command)) {
    const why = destructive(c)
    if (why) return why
  }
  return null
}
# trace

Hooks that keep a model's work traced and its context grounded in the project's decisions (ADRs 0005–0008). It depends on `mattpocock-skills`, which supplies the skills the work goes through.

## The guard

A PreToolUse hook on Bash. In every repository, and in every subagent, it refuses commands that destroy work or history:

- `git reset --hard`, `clean -f`, discarding checkouts, switches and restores
- force pushes, including `+refspec`
- `stash drop` / `clear`, `branch -D`
- `--no-verify`, `commit -n`, and overriding `core.hooksPath`
- `rm -r`

It parses the command the way a shell does. Text that only mentions a command is allowed, such as `rg 'rm -rf'` or a commit message. Commands inside `bash -c`, `$(...)` and heredocs fed to a shell are checked. It reads command text, so it is a seatbelt, not a sandbox.

## Compaction

The trace keeps state in GitHub, not in the context. To compact earlier, at about 300k tokens for example, use Claude Code's own `autoCompactWindow` setting. The plugin does not manage compaction.
